Date: Fri, 31 Dec 1993 11:00:19 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PPLs, Private Spaces, and "True Names"

Lord knows I don't speak for ExI, but:

Stuart Card writes:

> I am a little worried.  This space? What space? 
> "This is a space owned my (sic) ExI..."

The distribution channel itself creates a space, a place in
cyberspace. When we speak of "the List," this is the meaning. By
controlling who is on the List, who gets it, under what circumstance,
the ExI management has effectively "colonized" this part of the
conceptual space.

Others can of course create competing lists, recruiting in various
ways. They might even "steal" (not really) the ExI List membership
roster by writing down the e-mail addresses of all posters they see,
but this would be only a semi-active creation of a new space.
Eventually, a new list would have an active list and hence a new space
would've been colonized.

> What property rights, exactly, does ExI claim? And to what?
> Do my postings become the property of ExI? If it is not the
> postings to which ExI claims ownership, what else is there 
> 'here' to claim ownership of?

I beleve the "Welcome" message get sent to all new subscribers, and
even gets posted in toto to the List at various intervals. It goes
into gory detail about the ExI, the role of the List management, and
so on.

Altering some rules--evolution in action, don't you know--is natural.
Those who don't like the new rules are free to leave.

Those who think the new rules leave grounds for other interpretations
(as I think my TANSTAAFL fee schedule is a fully legal interpretation)
are of course free to test their interpretations, in which case the
ExI can remove them from the List or suggest modifications. Seems
reasonable to me.

In these privately-created spaces (ultimately to lead to full virtual
realities), the rules are made by the owners. Eventually these spaces
will be created with strong crypto present in key areas, so that, for
example, the "owners" of the space (the holders of the keys, the
security mechanisms, the list of allowable identities onto the list)
are not even identifiable.

Read "True Names" by Vernor Vinge for a compelling vision of this. 

"He who has the tools makes the rules."

--Tim May


