Date: Fri, 31 Dec 1993 12:09:48 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Meta: Robust vs. Weak Systems, Exploiting Loopholes

(My apologies for "forgetting" to put a "Meta:" in front of my last
posting, on PPLs and Private Spaces.)

Though I just defended the "private space" nature of the List in my
last post, it should come as no surprise that I have some doubts about
the current experiment.

Let me be brief, even telegraphic:

* "Robust" systems are resistant to attacks on the rule sets. The real
world, for example, is the epitome of robustness...there is no "legal
move" using the laws of physics, math, etc., that makes the whole
thing fall apart.

* "Weak" systems are easy prey for attacks on the rule sets, that is,
using legal moves to demolish the system. Certain games are completely
vulnerable to simple strategies that always win. Thus, we can say that
"tick-tack-toe" is "weak" and Go and chess are "robust." (There are
interesting questions concerning intelligence of the players,
computational power, look-ahead depth, etc., which I won't address
here.)

Formal (axiomatic) systems may be robust or weak in the same way. 

Weak systems often collapse when a "loophole" is found which
demolishes the entire structure. Can one tell set of axioms make a
robust system and what set make a weak system? This is
system-building, and the Federalist Papers are the usual place to
start looking. And some metamathematics, presumably unknown to the
Founders and most lawyers, may figure in (yes, Godel's Theorem,
especially as interpreted by Chaitin and Kolmogorov).

The U.S. Constitutional system (aplogies to the non-American readers
out there) is remarkably robust as such formal systems go, being
tested constantly by lawsuits, criminal trials, the judgements of tens
of thousands of judges, etc. None of the challenges has yet demolished
the entire system, the War of the Rebellion (Civil War, popularly)
probably came closest (and the South _should_ have been allowed to
seceed, say I).

The current experiment with PPL on this List falls into "weak"
category. Probably because of the lack of richness, the lack of
real-world incentives that money, time, and history can bring.

Too many loopholes exist and will demolish this "toy system"
forthwith. Toys usually break soon after Christmas, so we're on schedule.

To give an example. Mr. May's Greater Extropia, my PPL, has been duly
sanctioned by the formal rules of the ExI management. But I just
published my legal fees---surely we are allowed to set our legal fees?
To have "price controls" and laws against "price-gouging" seems most
un-Extropian, and the laws make no mention of this---and these fees
essentially mean nobody will ever bother me with legal charges.

This nukes the possibility of mediation. (But not because I "refuse"
to mediate, only because my legal fees are beyond the reach of most
people on the List!)

(I happen to think this is a good thing. And I think Harry was being
overly generous with his time when he handled all those crazy lawsuits
people filed against each other free of charge.)

And so it goes with Nick's "Wolf's Head" PPL, with the "PPL-Self" that
may or may not exist, etc.

Let me add here that sometimes weak, toy systems are propped up by a
"gentleman's agreement" that loopholes not be exploited, that the
participants "play along" and ignore the weaknesses. I call these
systems "quasi-robust." They aren't really robust, but so long as
everyone agrees not to exploit certain strategies or certain
loopholes, they mostly act in a reasonably robust way.

I suspect that the carping about the PPLs that are allowed, the
"spirit" of the proposals, etc., are all related to trying to reach
some consensus on what these "meta-rules" or gentleman's agreements
should be. (And purists of metamathematics and philosophy will note
that all formal systems appear to have at least some meta-rules, such
as the rules governing interpretion of the axioms. Russell and
Whitehead tried to get rid of this, and failed. In terms of this list,
such meta-rules are the various ideas, definitions, and
interpretations surrounding the core ideas. Someone who asks "But how
do you define "law"?" and proceeds into an infinite regress into more
and more definitions has found a "meta-rule loophole" and cannot be
dealt with further.)

So is it all hopeless, are there no robust systems?

Property ownership and denial of access turns out to be quite robust.
This List basically works not because of a formal (and flawed, viz.
the point above) structure but because anyone who shows himself to be
unwelcome enough will simply be kicked off. No appeals, no higher
rules, no loopholes to exploit.

That's robustness.

Private produced law, or locality of reference, is a good idea--we do
it everyday, in our corporations, familes, and lists like this--but to
do it in a closed environment with the expectation that loopholes will
not be exploited is misguided.

A fascinating topic. I suspect there are deep--and not yet explored,
that I know of--connections between formal systems, open vs. closed
systems, evolution, market processes, and information theory.

--Tim May


