Date: Fri, 31 Dec 1993 12:52:42 -0800 (PST)
From: Oliver Seiler <oseiler@unixg.ubc.ca>
Subject: Khmer Vert - Seal Rant

On Fri, 31 Dec 1993, Timothy C. May wrote:
> 
> The environmental issues are unresolved, I think. Rand never had to
> deal with it (except for her trademark "Factories belching smoke are
> the symbol of man's control over nature"), and, as I recall,
> "Machinery of Freedom" did not dwell on issues of overfishing of the
> oceans or loss of habitat or the ozone layer in detail. (David can
> correct me if I've misremembered this.)

> Anyway, there are lots of questions, and not a lot of answers, as I
> see it. While I'm a libertarian, anarchocapitalist in all
> person-to-person dealings, I have more doubts about how to handle
> damage to the "environment" and to (possibly) endangered species.
> 
> The issues are familiar: 
> 
> - property rights for things that have historically not been
> considered property (air, oceans, species, etc.). Challenging.

And how can someone claim ownership of these? Hard to say, but I'm inclined
to say that ownership of property should be thought of more by the amount
of work someone (or some entity has put into it). Although this is vague, it
pretty much says no one owns these "natural" things and therefor shouldn't
assume they can do with them what they will. This also doesn't say they
can't. If I want to mine the ocean floor of some ore, and in the process
screw up some other person's fish harvest (that they were already doing),
then that other person should be able to file for damages against me (how
this is done, discovered, etc. is up for debate - again this is just the
way I feel about things)...

If I however colonize a big section of Mars (somehow) and "create" an
ocean (again somehow), then I think it could be said that if I didn't affect
other people's projects (say I was the first one there), then that ocean
is mine, and people who want to use it in theory have to come to me...

> - can libertarians support "rationing" of a service or commodity? (The
> thought generally is repugnant....I try to tell my neighbors that we
> don't _need_ water rationing in California, so long as the market is
> allowed to work. My problem here is that since no one owns the supply
> of snow leopards or seal pups or clean air, how is a market ever
> established?)

But to me this is a problem with the ease to which the user's of these 
resources can affect things. If I suddenly find that the increased pollution
in the air is a result of car pollution, how do I change this? I should be
able to sue the manufacturer's of the vehicles for failing to ensure that
their products aren't health hazards, and so, endanger my health (or would I
sue the driver of such a vehicle?) Of course, this would completely screw up
the legal system I think, since everyone would start filing charges. This
couldn't be done retroactively.

(The above is of course completely imaginary - although I wish it wasn't)

Besides, who would ration things that nobody owns? I am really annoyed with
my government for selling water to the US. I don't think it is their's to
sell (no anyones) since nobody owns it. People are already in general, reliant
on having water up here, but the effects of selling the water to the south
will also affect those north of the border who are already using it. If all
the current user's agreed to sell, and they got all the money, then go right
to it, since I'm not going to be affected directly. Anybody else who though
they were affected by their decision could sue, or try to get a part of that
money, but this isn't currently possible...

Simply what I'm saying (and which has been discussed elsewhere I'm sure) is
that the current user's of a "nonowned" resource become the effective owners
in cases where damages may be claimed. I just don't think that going from a
system of public (ie. government "owned"/"stolen") property going to a
system where I can sue somebody for polluting my air (and where everyone else
can do the same thing) with their car, would work nicely...

It goes without saying that everything I've said is really simplistic, so don't
flame me about that (this is all off the top of my head, with very little
though put into it, since I haven't really thought a whole lot about these
issues). I can huge problems with this. Who owns land? If I build a house on
some land, what is really mine? How big is my property? (I can think of one
answer. If you can protect it, it's yours... not nice, but really true.)

> - punishment may have to be much more than the reparations for a
> crime. In the recent (and fizzled out, this time) thread on
> imprisonment, several folks averred that libertarians only support
> incarceration to prevent continuing crime, and that reparations in the
> amount of the damages are all that can be "demanded." Well, the
> burglar who gets caught once per 100 jobs will not be deterred by
> having to "compensate" (give back the loot) the victim for the one
> time out of 100 he gets caught. No, punishment may have to be much
> greater. (I recall Friedman suggesting a 3x ratio in MoF, but no
> strong reasons were given.)

Can one really just arbitrarily say that we should punish a criminal more than
his crime was actually worth because he possibly did more that weren't known?
(well, yes we could... we could also arbitrarily kill him or put him away for
life as well.) I think recouping all costs to the victim (real costs, such as
medical costs, court costs) as well as paying for all costs associated with
imprisonment is necessary. Altruistic behaviour towards criminals is stupid
(such as guaranteed food, clothing, etc.) although is someone feels like doing
it, they can go right ahead.

I haven't thought a lot about punishment of rights-breakers (I'm trying to get
away from the word criminal since it seems to me to be a loaded term, full of
state rhetoric), but it seems to me more effort should be placed into making
crime not pay - short term solutions (for the current system) being such
things as letting people legally carry firearms, which would require we get
away from the "Guns bad!" attitude which is popular (the carrying of guns is
less of an issue for the US than for in Canada, where I think we'll be seeing
a large rise in crimes with guns present - I'm already seeing this in
Vancouver), prison's shouldn't be paid for by the public, getting rid of the
victimless crime laws which are clogging the prisons we have.

> - as this point relates to poachers and other killers of, say,
> condors and Bengal tigers, the punishment may have to be much more
> severe than, say, paying a $500 fine or being rapped on the knuckles.
> What this should be, who might administer it, and on what basis it
> derives from, are all issues I haven't begun to resolve.

True. If the animals are owned, the owners can kill the poachers (hell, anyone
can kill the poachers. The animals would probably enjoy the change in diet)

The problem seems to be that the poacher's don't know a lot about real free
market economics, and that they are killing there own market. (I assume that
the market for poached products like ivory is competitive.) Of course it's the
short term profits to be made that count for these people. Is there any way
to make it more profitable, in the short and long run, to not poach? Is there
a way to make the market for these products fall? (An interesting method would
be to make edible products poisonous. If this could be done without harming the
animal, I think the market would, quite literally, drop - say a neurotoxin
introduced into a rhino's system which concentrates in their horn. Powdered it
kill's the user's quite painfully... Cruel? Sure! But if you don't want your
rhino's to be killed... Quite a business could be made by some enterprising
bio-tech company...)

> This is all I'll say for now. I don't have many answers.

That's okay, not a lot of people do, since the issues are so all encompassing.
(I know I don't)...

> A while back, Max More opined that the Greens would be the next
> totalitarian movement. He suggested a campaign by Extropians against
> this movement. I haven't heard much more about this since.

Also a great way to get Extropians a target by the new generation of young
hippie eco fascists...
 
> Perhaps Extropians could work on solutions which address the very real
> species and environmental problems, as well, thus providing a more
> libertarian, extropic alternative to those Greens who want a return to
> a pastoral, deindustrialized world.

We should all definitely talk about it more, since there is definitely a void
to be filled. Ownership of things like air and the oceans don't make a lot of
sense to people (not to me either), but this thinking (that no one owns these
things at all) bring about all sorts of problems with protection of the
environment, species, etc. The question seems to become: At what point does
exploitation of a resource become wrong? (I mean exploitation here as using
a resource for doing useful things). At what point does waste become serious
pollution (face it, carbon dioxide is waste. In large amounts we would die
from it, but we breath it out all the time. When does exhaust become "bad"?)

